Compared to the second quarter, the acceleration in real GDP in the third quarter primarily reflected an
acceleration in investment and an upturn in government spending that were partly offset by a downturn
in exports. Imports increased more in the third quarter than in the second quarter.

Real final sales to private domestic purchasers, the sum of consumer spending and gross private fixed
investment, increased 5.8 percent in the third quarter, revised up 0.9 percentage point from the
previous estimate.

The price index for gross domestic purchases increased 2.7 percent in the third quarter, revised down
0.3 percentage point from the previous estimate. The personal consumption expenditures (PCE) price
index increased 2.1 percent, and the PCE price index, excluding food and energy increased 2.5 percent,
both the same as previously estimated.

