Technical Notes
Sources of revision to real GDP
Real GDP increased at an annual rate of 4.3 percent (1.1 percent at a quarterly rate1) in the third
quarter, an upward revision of 0.9 percentage point from the previous estimate, primarily reflecting
upward revisions to investment and consumer spending.
• The revision to investment was led by an upward revision to intellectual property products,
notably software, based primarily on revisions to the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics’ Producer
Price Index for system and application software publishing, excluding maintenance, tech support
and games.
• The revision to consumer spending reflected an upward revision to goods that was partly offset
by a downward revision to services.
o Within goods, the upward revision was led by other nondurable goods (mainly
pharmaceuticals), recreational goods and vehicles (mainly computer software and
accessories), and gasoline and other energy goods, based on revised data from the U.S.
Census Bureau Monthly Retail Trade Survey and new and revised data from the U.S.
Department of Energy.
o Within services, the largest contributor to the downward revision was health care (both
hospital and nursing home services and outpatient services), based on newly available
third-quarter U.S. Census Bureau Quarterly Services Survey data.
More information on the source data and BEA assumptions that underlie the third-quarter estimate is
shown in the key source data and assumptions table.

